  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Haridas vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:18001) on 24 April, 2024 Author: Manoj Kumar Garg Bench: Manoj Kumar Garg [2024:Rj-Jd:18001] High Court Of Judicature For Rajasthan At Jodhpur S.B. Criminal Revision Petition No. 488/2001 Haridas S/o Shri Dev Kishan, By caste Rathi, R/o Sadar Bazar, Pokaran, District Jaisalmer.",
    "facts": "Bereft of elaborate details, facts relevant and essential for disposal of the instant criminal revision are that on 21.05.1996 Food Inspector Bhanwarlal Purohit, went to the shop of M/s Chunilal Ghanshyam Das and took samples of red chilli powder [2024:RJ-JD:18001] (2 of 5) [CRLR-488/2001] from the shop of the petitioner. After following due procedure, the samples were sent for examination and the same were found to be adulterated. 3. The Learned Magistrate framed the charge against the petitioner for the offence under Section 7/16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act and upon denial of guilt by him, commenced the trial. The accused, upon being confronted with the prosecution allegations, in his statement under Section 313 CrPC, denied the allegations and claimed to be innocent. Hence, this revision petition is filed before this court.",
    "arguments": "4. After arguing the case on merits to some extent, learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submits that he will not assail conviction of the petitioner and confines his arguments to the alternative prayer of reduction of the sentence awarded by the trial court. He submits that the incident in the present case pertains to the year 1996. The petitioner was 42 years of age at that time. He was not having any criminal antecedents and it was the first criminal case registered against him. No adverse remark has been passed over his conduct except the impugned judgment. With these submissions, learned counsel prays that by taking a lenient view, the sentence awarded to the petitioner may be reduced to the period already undergone.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. After arguing the case on merits to some extent, learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submits that he will not assail conviction of the petitioner and confines his arguments to the alternative prayer of reduction of the sentence awarded by the trial court. He submits that the incident in the present case pertains to the year 1996. The petitioner was 42 years of age at that time. He was not having any criminal antecedents and it was the first criminal case registered against him. No adverse remark has been passed over his conduct except the impugned judgment. With these submissions, learned counsel prays that by taking a lenient view, the sentence awarded to the petitioner may be reduced to the period already undergone.",
